course: dictionary
lesson: 2560
title: Many meanings, one word
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning, and context tells you which one fits. Read the words around the word. «Toad shook his head, raised one paw in mild protest, and offered them delicacies and polite small-talk.» Offered is the clue: you offer food to guests, so delicacies here means special, tasty food.
explain: Delicacy can also mean being fine, light and easily broken, or care when a matter is difficult or embarrassing. When you meet delicacy, try each meaning in the sentence and see which one still makes sense.
explain: The quickest check is to swap your meaning in. «Toad offered them special, tasty food» makes sense, so you have the right meaning.
fact: A delicacy is a special, unusual or expensive food that people think is very tasty.
fact: Delicacy can also mean being fine, light or easily broken.
fact: Delicacy can mean care and tact when a matter is difficult or embarrassing.
fact: «A matter of some delicacy» means a matter that must be handled carefully.
fact: Countries have their own delicacies: snails in France, haggis in Scotland, and pavlova in Australia and New Zealand.
exercise: «Toad offered them delicacies and polite small-talk.» What are delicacies here? | Rude words said loudly | *Special, tasty food | A type of long, thin boat | Hard, broken pieces of glass
exercise: «The news was a matter of some delicacy, so the teacher spoke quietly and carefully.» What does delicacy mean here? | Being light and easily broken | Being very loud | Very tasty food | *Needing careful, tactful handling
exercise: Which is the best example of a delicacy? | A cracked, empty cup | A bowl of plain water | *A rare, expensive dish that people think is very tasty | A heavy stone on the path
test: «The wine glass was thin and breakable, and its delicacy showed in the way it caught the light.» What does delicacy mean? | Rude behaviour | Careful handling of an awkward subject | *Fine, light and easily broken | Very tasty food
test: Mrs Patel says, «Let's not raise that subject, it's a matter of some delicacy.» What does she mean? | The subject is about food for a picnic | The subject is very loud and noisy | *The subject must be handled carefully and tactfully | The subject is long and boring
exam: Which sentence uses delicacy to mean a special, tasty food? | «She handled the question with great delicacy.» | «The thin ice showed its delicacy as it cracked.» | «The matter needed delicacy before anyone spoke.» | *«The restaurant served rare delicacies from around the world.»
